[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Transaction Management and ACID Compliance in ETL Targets"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Ensuring data integrity is paramount in modern data warehousing and ETL (Extract, Transform, Load) pipelines. When loading data into target databases or data lakes, developers must understand how transaction management and ACID properties guarantee reliable and consistent state transitions."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding ACID Properties in ETL Loads"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"ACID stands for Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, and Durability. In the context of ETL targets, these properties dictate how data batches are written and committed:"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Atomicity: Ensures that all operations within an ETL load batch succeed together or fail completely. Partial loads are rolled back to prevent dirty data."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Consistency: Guarantees that the target database moves from one valid state to another, strictly adhering to defined schemas, foreign keys, and unique constraints."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Isolation: Manages concurrent ETL processes and user queries, ensuring intermediate states of a load are not visible until the transaction explicitly commits."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Durability: Once an ETL batch commits, the changes are permanently saved, even in the event of subsequent system crashes or power failures."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Configuring Target Commit Intervals"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"In high-volume ETL pipelines, committing every single row severely degrades performance. Conversely, committing only at the very end of a massive load risks exhausting transaction log space. Configuring optimal commit intervals balances throughput and recovery time."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"BEGIN TRANSACTION;
SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL READ COMMITTED;

-- Bulk insert staged ETL data
INSERT INTO target_table (id, customer_name, updated_at)
SELECT staging_id, staging_name, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
FROM staging_table;

COMMIT;"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Handling Failures and Rollback Strategies"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"When an ETL target rejects a row due to constraint violations or network timeouts, the pipeline must decide whether to fail the entire batch or route bad records to an error table. Using exception handling blocks prevents pipeline crashes."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"BEGIN TRY
    BEGIN TRANSACTION;
    -- ETL load operations
    UPDATE target_table SET status = 'Processed' WHERE batch_id = 101;
    COMMIT TRANSACTION;
END TRY
BEGIN CATCH
    IF @@TRANCOUNT > 0
        ROLLBACK TRANSACTION;
    -- Log error details
    INSERT INTO etl_error_log (error_message, error_time) VALUES (ERROR_MESSAGE(), GETDATE());
END CATCH;"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Modern Data Lakes and Warehouses"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Modern cloud architectures use table formats like Apache Iceberg, Delta Lake, and Apache Hudi to bring ACID transaction guarantees to object storage. When designing ETL targets, leverage these technologies to support time-travel, concurrent reads and writes, and atomic schema evolution."}]
